{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqh0h2hktq7","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"Harness claude -p on Windows: deliver prompt via stdin, resolve CLI with shutil which","summary":"Found building the agent-memory probe runner (bench/agent-memory/probe_runner.py) on the embeddable Python 3.12 Windows box. Two concrete constraints for any stdlib harness that spawns `claude -p`: (1) `claude` is a .cmd shim (claude.CMD under AppData/Roaming/npm). subprocess.run with shell=False and a bare name \"claude\" fails with WinError 2 because PATHEXT .cmd resolution is NOT done for bare names — use shutil.which(\"claude\") which returns the resolvable .CMD path. (2) Passing a long / multi-line prompt as a POSITIONAL CLI arg gets mangled by cmd.exe arg parsing: observed live, medium and long positional prompts (with apostrophes, braces, quotes) arrived truncated or empty at the model — the model replied \"I don't see the question\". Deliver the prompt on STDIN instead: subprocess.run([claude,'-p','--model',m,'--output-format','text','--allowedTools',''], input=prompt, text=True, encoding='utf-8'). `claude -p` with NO positional reads the prompt from stdin, byte-clean regardless of length/special chars. Also force encoding='utf-8' on the subprocess (box default cp1252 mojibakes non-ASCII). The swe-bench zonoid_memory.py reference passed the prompt positionally — that pattern is unsafe for long prompts on Windows.","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-16T19:04:32.216Z","vec":[0.002530294470489025,-0.015672367066144943,-0.09401871263980865,0.02419188618659973,-0.05465451255440712,-0.02610899694263935,-0.020753690972924232,0.11109455674886703,0.00640697218477726,0.002192343585193157,-0.05580878257751465,-0.06120658293366432,0.06592069566249847,0.013284817337989807,-0.012489243410527706,-0.029571328312158585,0.01361068058758974,-0.03166019171476364,-0.021387683227658272,-0.0005642438773065805,0.055436473339796066,0.024036988615989685,0.04440902918577194,-0.011635328643023968,-0.1051